From fc698b2aa583dfc6599f89f84994957024aa1520 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Reixo <125610085+Reixo0@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 00:04:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 08/39] power: supply: qcom_battmgr: Ignore unsolicited
 messages on SM8350-style firmware

Kaanapali firmware sends messages with opcode 0x101 every few seconds.
The SM8350-style callback logged a warning for each, flooding the kernel
log, and then completed battmgr->ack as if a reply had arrived. That
wakes up whichever request is pending before its real reply, which then
returns stale data: on a Nubia RedMagic 11 Pro charge_counter read 0.

Warn once about unknown messages and don't complete the pending request
for them. The SC8280XP-style callback has the same pattern but wasn't
changed here, for lack of a device to test it on.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
index 676b9a98d..594063b62 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
@@ -1588,8 +1588,13 @@ static void qcom_battmgr_sm8350_callback(struct qcom_battmgr *battmgr,
 		battmgr->error = 0;
 		break;
 	default:
-		dev_warn(battmgr->dev, "unknown message %#x\n", opcode);
-		break;
+		/*
+		 * Newer firmware (e.g. Kaanapali) sends unsolicited messages,
+		 * such as 0x101, every few seconds. They aren't a reply to a
+		 * request, so they must not complete a pending one.
+		 */
+		dev_warn_once(battmgr->dev, "unknown message %#x\n", opcode);
+		return;
 	}
 
 out_complete:
-- 
2.56.0

